 "meta_desc": "Class 7 Finding the Unknown worksheet (Ganita Prakash Part 2, Chapter 7): one-step and two-step equations, checking solutions, forming equations and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Keep the balance: do the same to both sides",
 "key_sub": "Undo + and − first, then × and ÷.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>An <b>equation</b> says two expressions are equal, like 3x + 5 = 20. Solving it means finding the value of the unknown that makes it true.</p><p>Keep it balanced: do the same to both sides. 3x + 5 = 20 → 3x = 15 (subtract 5) → x = 5 (divide by 3). Check: 3 × 5 + 5 = 20.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 4x − 7 = 21.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4x = 28</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x = 28 ÷ 4</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: x = 7</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve x/3 + 2 = 9.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x/3 = 7</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x = 21</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: x = 21</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Doing an operation to one side only.</li><li>Subtracting the number in front of x instead of dividing.</li><li>Adding before dividing in the wrong order (3x + 5 = 20 as x = 25 ÷ 3).</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does it mean to solve an equation?",
   "a": "To find the value of the unknown that makes both sides equal."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you keep an equation balanced?",
   "a": "Whatever you do to one side — add, subtract, multiply or divide — do the same to the other side."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can you check your answer?",
   "a": "Put the value back into the equation; both sides should be equal."
  }
